<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  The LaunchDaemon that keeps the forward up.

  A template: cmd/tunnel.zsh substitutes the @TOKENS@ at install time and
  validates the result with plutil before it is written, because a malformed
  plist is rejected by launchd with a message that says nothing useful.

  **A daemon, not an agent.** Two reasons, and only the second is about
  privilege. Time Machine backs up whether or not anyone is logged in, and a
  LaunchAgent stops at logout - so an agent would silently turn "hourly
  backups" into "backups while you are at the keyboard". And the forward has to
  bind port 445, which needs root, because `tmutil setdestination` refuses to
  validate a destination on any other port.

  **Nothing secret is in here.** The arguments are a host, a user name and two
  paths. The key itself sits next to this file at mode 0600.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>@LABEL@</string>

  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>@HELPER@</string>
    <string>--host</string>
    <string>@HOST@</string>
    <string>--user</string>
    <string>@USER@</string>
    <string>--key</string>
    <string>@KEY@</string>
    <string>--known-hosts</string>
    <string>@KNOWN_HOSTS@</string>
    <string>--bind</string>
    <string>@BIND@</string>
  </array>

  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <true/>

  <!--
    KeepAlive with no conditions: this connection is the backup path, and there
    is no state in which it should be down. A closed laptop, a changed address
    or a rebooted appliance all end the same way - ssh exits and launchd starts
    it again.

    ThrottleInterval raises launchd's ten-second floor to thirty. The failure
    this guards against is an appliance that is up but not accepting the
    forward, where a ten-second retry loop burns the connection budget and
    fills the log. Thirty seconds is still well inside Time Machine's hourly
    cycle, so a tunnel that recovers costs no backup.
  -->
  <key>KeepAlive</key>
  <true/>
  <key>ThrottleInterval</key>
  <integer>30</integer>

  <!--
    ssh says nothing at all when it is working, so anything here is a fault
    worth keeping. The file is append-only: nothing rotates it in 0.1.0, which
    installs no newsyslog configuration. A tunnel that keeps failing adds ssh's
    error output on each restart, and restarts at most once per
    ThrottleInterval.
  -->
  <key>StandardOutPath</key>
  <string>@LOG@</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
  <string>@LOG@</string>

  <!--
    No ProcessType: the default is what is wanted. Background would have
    launchd deprioritise the job's I/O and CPU, and this job is carrying every
    byte of every backup.
  -->
</dict>
</plist>
